            "Endogenous variables are the cornerstone of econometric models, serving as the internal instruments through which a model explains the causal relationships within an economic system. Unlike exogenous variables, which are external factors not influenced by the system's dynamics, endogenous variables are shaped by the system itself and, in turn, influence other variables within the same system. This intrinsic interdependence is what makes the study of endogenous variables both fascinating and complex. They are the pulse of the model, indicating how internal changes can propagate through the system, leading to significant economic outcomes.",
