            "The concept of endogeneity has long been a cornerstone in the study of economics, shaping our understanding of how internal factors within an economic system can influence outcomes in ways that are often complex and interdependent. As we look to the future, the role of endogenous variables in economic analysis is poised to become even more significant. The increasing availability of granular data, advancements in econometric methods, and the growing complexity of global economic interactions all point to a future where endogeneity will be an even more critical factor in shaping economic outcomes.",
            "From the perspective of data availability, the digital age has ushered in an era of 'big data', providing economists with a wealth of information that was previously inaccessible. This abundance of data allows for a more nuanced analysis of endogenous relationships, enabling researchers to uncover patterns and correlations that were once obscured by data limitations.",
            "Advancements in econometric methods have also expanded our capacity to address endogeneity. Techniques such as instrumental variables, difference-in-differences, and regression discontinuity designs have become more sophisticated, allowing economists to better identify causal relationships and reduce bias in their estimates.",
